<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">August von Gneisenau</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1760–1831</span> · Also known as August Neidhardt von Gneisenau</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">August Neidhardt von Gneisenau was a Prussian field marshal and military reformer. After defending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolobrzeg">Kolberg</a> in 1807 he worked with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerhard-von-scharnhorst">Scharnhorst</a> to rebuild the Prussian army after its defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gebhard-leberecht-von-blucher">Blücher</a>'s chief of staff in 1813–1815 he planned the campaigns that led to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterloo">Waterloo</a>, where he directed the Prussian march to the battlefield after Ligny.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/august-von-gneisenau.jpg" alt="August von Gneisenau" width="320" height="404" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Dawe · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:George_Dawe,_Field_Marshal_August_Neidhardt,_Count_of_Gneisenau_(1760%E2%80%931831),_1818.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
